<section class="section"><div class="wrap"><div class="panel">
  <div class="eyebrow">The Book</div>
  <h1 class="book-title" style="font-size:clamp(30px,4.5vw,44px);margin:0">{{ site.title }}</h1>
  <p class="subtitle-line">{{ site.subtitle }}</p>
  <p class="book-byline">by {{ site.author }}</p>

  <p class="lead"><strong>You don't have a time-management problem. You have a too-many-open-doors problem.</strong></p>
  <p>Everywhere you look, someone is doing more. More projects, more side hustles, more 5 a.m. routines, more "optimised" everything. Open any feed and a stranger has already read fifty books this year, launched a company, and run a marathon, all before breakfast. And now that AI has made almost any idea buildable in a weekend, the pressure to chase every one of them at once has never been higher. So you say yes to everything. You spread yourself thin. You keep every door open, just in case. And you end up busy, tired, and quietly wondering why a life that looks so full on paper feels so hollow to live.</p>
  <p><em>Selective Ambition</em> is the antidote to that feeling.</p>
  <p>This is not another productivity system, and it is not a plan to cram more into your day. It is the case for the opposite: caring deeply about fewer things, and letting the rest go on purpose. It is the Joy of Missing Out, JOMO, not as a throwaway hashtag but as a genuine way of choosing, working, and finally feeling like yourself again. Because the problem was never your discipline, your morning routine, or your time management. The problem is a world that hands you infinite options and then calls the exhaustion that follows "ambition."</p>
  <p>Drawing on hard-won personal experience and the science of attention, comparison, and choice, {{ site.author }} shows why more options have quietly made us less happy, and how one deliberate decision changes everything: to stop chasing every open door and walk, on purpose, through the one that actually matters.</p>
  <p><em>Selective Ambition</em> moves from the rush that wears us down, through the pause that wakes us up, to the choices that set us free and the quiet joy waiting on the other side. It is honest about how hard this is, especially when your job, your family, or your bills mean you cannot simply walk away. So alongside the ideas you will find tools you can use the same afternoon: a way to audit the inputs feeding your anxiety, a clear framework for the intentional no, and small "five per cent shifts" for the seasons when quitting is not an option.</p>

  <div class="rule"><span></span></div>
